        'description' => 'Normal sleep architecture (NREM Stages N1-N3, REM sleep), polysomnography parameters, and the suprachiasmatic nucleus circadian clock.',
        'overview' => "Understanding sleep disorders requires an exact command of normal neurobiological sleep architecture and polysomnography. Human sleep is a dynamic, highly structured neurobiological process organized into cyclical 90-to-120-minute ultradian cycles that alternate between Non-Rapid Eye Movement (NREM) sleep and Rapid Eye Movement (REM) sleep across a typical 7-to-8-hour nocturnal period.\n\nNREM sleep comprises three distinct stages: Stage N1 (light transitional sleep, characterized by alpha attenuation and theta waves); Stage N2 (deeper sleep, accounting for ~50% of total sleep time, electrophysiologically defined by sleep spindles and K-complexes); and Stage N3 (slow-wave sleep or delta sleep, characterized by high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves > 75 uV), which mediates somatic physical restoration, growth hormone secretion, and synaptic down-scaling. In contrast, REM sleep (paradoxical sleep) is characterized by low-voltage desynchronized EEG, rapid eye movements, autonomic variability, vivid dreaming, and profound somatic muscle atonia mediated by glycine/GABA inhibition of spinal motor neurons.\n\nCircadian rhythmicity is governed by the master biological clock in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the anterior hypothalamus, which receives direct photic input via the retinohypothalamic tract and regulates pineal gland melatonin synthesis. In DSM-5, Sleep-Wake Disorders were reorganized into an independent chapter that emphasizes clinical sleep medicine parameters, uncoupling sleep pathology from secondary mental illness.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Polysomnographic and Sleep Parameters under DSM-5:\n\n1. Polysomnography (PSG) Metrics:\n• Sleep Latency: Normal time to sleep onset is 10-20 minutes (>30 minutes indicates sleep onset insomnia).\n• REM Latency: Normal time from sleep onset to first REM period is 90-120 minutes (REM latency <= 15 minutes is pathognomonic for Narcolepsy or Major Depression).\n• Slow-Wave Sleep (N3): Diminishes progressively with advancing age and major depressive illness.\n• Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI): Number of apneas/hypopneas per hour of sleep (AHI >= 5 with symptoms, or AHI >= 15 alone, confirms Obstructive Sleep Apnea).\n\n2. The Two-Process Model of Sleep Regulation:\n• Process S (Homeostatic Sleep Drive): Accumulates with prolonged wakefulness (mediated by extracellular adenosine accumulation in the basal forebrain; antagonized by caffeine).\n• Process C (Circadian Drive): Independent 24-hour alerting signal generated by the suprachiasmatic nucleus.",
        'specifiers' => "Sleep disorders specify whether the disturbance is: 'Episodic' (lasts at least 1 month but less than 3 months), 'Persistent' (lasts 3 months or longer), or 'Recurrent' (two or more episodes within 1 year).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Primary Sleep Disorders vs. Sleep Disruption Secondary to Psychiatric Illness: In MDD, sleep disruption features shortened REM latency and decreased slow-wave sleep; in GAD, difficulty initiating sleep is driven by cognitive worry; in Primary Insomnia, the sleep complaint is independent.\n\nCircadian Rhythm Sleep-Wake Disorders: Delayed Sleep Phase Type (night owls who cannot fall asleep until 3 AM and wake at 11 AM) vs. Advanced Sleep Phase Type (early birds who sleep at 7 PM and wake at 3 AM).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 24-year-old student evaluated for severe daytime sleepiness. Overnight polysomnography demonstrates normal nocturnal sleep efficiency, but during a daytime Multiple Sleep Latency Test (MSLT), the student falls asleep in an average of 2.5 minutes (severe sleepiness, normal >10 min) and exhibits three Sleep-Onset REM Periods (SOREMPs).\n\nBecause the student enters REM sleep within minutes of daytime naps, the physiological finding demonstrates loss of normal REM gating mechanisms, providing objective confirmatory evidence for Narcolepsy.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Stage N2 sleep features sleep spindles and K-complexes (50% of sleep). Stage N3 (delta sleep) mediates physical restoration and decreases with age. REM sleep features muscle atonia, desynchronized EEG, and vivid dreaming. The master circadian clock is the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus (SCN). Extracellular adenosine drives homeostatic sleep pressure (Process S). Sleep-onset REM periods (SOREMPs) with shortened REM latency (<= 15 min) indicate Narcolepsy.",
