    2 => [
        'title' => 'Insomnia Disorder (Onset, Maintenance, Early Awakening; 3 Months)',
        'slug' => 'insomnia-disorder-criteria',
        'description' => 'Sleep dissatisfaction, 3 nights/week for >= 3 months threshold, adequate opportunity requirement, and CBT-I first-line efficacy.',
        'overview' => "Insomnia Disorder is the most prevalent sleep-wake disorder in clinical practice, characterized by a persistent dissatisfaction with sleep quantity or quality, accompanied by difficulty initiating sleep, difficulty maintaining sleep, or early-morning awakening with inability to return to sleep. A foundational conceptual shift in DSM-5 was the complete elimination of the historical distinction between 'primary insomnia' and 'secondary insomnia'.\n\nUnder DSM-IV, insomnia was frequently dismissed as a mere symptom of comorbid depression, anxiety, or medical illness ('secondary insomnia'). DSM-5 established Insomnia Disorder as an independent, standalone diagnosis that is coded concurrently with any coexisting medical, psychiatric, or sleep condition. Research definitively demonstrated that chronic insomnia follows an independent pathophysiological course, alters neuroplasticity, and requires targeted clinical treatment rather than expecting sleep to automatically normalize with antidepressant therapy alone.\n\nTo meet DSM-5 diagnostic criteria, insomnia must occur at least three nights per week, persist for at least three consecutive months, and occur despite adequate opportunity and circumstances for sleep. This 'adequate opportunity' requirement ensures that sleep loss due to work shifts, family demands, or noisy environments is not misdiagnosed as Insomnia Disorder.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A predominant complaint of dissatisfaction with sleep quantity or quality, associated with one (or more) of the following symptoms:\n1. Difficulty initiating sleep (sleep onset latency > 30 minutes; in children, may manifest as difficulty without caregiver intervention).\n2. Difficulty maintaining sleep, characterized by frequent awakenings or problems returning to sleep after awakenings (wake after sleep onset > 30 minutes).\n3. Early-morning awakening with inability to return to sleep (waking >= 30 minutes before desired time and before total sleep reaches 6.5 hours).\n\nCriterion B: The sleep disturbance causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, educational, academic, behavioral, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion C: The sleep difficulty occurs at least 3 nights per week.\n\nCriterion D: The sleep difficulty is present for at least 3 months.\n\nCriterion E: The sleep difficulty occurs despite adequate opportunity for sleep.\n\nCriterion F: The insomnia is not better explained by and does not occur exclusively during another sleep-wake disorder (e.g., narcolepsy, breathing-related sleep disorder, circadian rhythm disorder, parasomnia).\n\nCriterion G and H: Not attributable to physiological effects of a substance and not better explained by coexisting mental or medical conditions.",
        'specifiers' => "Course Specifiers: 'Episodic' (symptoms last at least 1 month but less than 3 months); 'Persistent' (symptoms last 3 months or longer); or 'Recurrent' (two or more episodes within the space of 1 year).\n\nComorbidity Specifiers: 'With non-sleep mental disorder comorbidity' (e.g., Major Depressive Disorder), 'With other medical condition comorbidity', or 'With other sleep disorder comorbidity'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Insomnia Disorder vs. Sleep Restriction / Environmental Sleep Deprivation: Individuals who work two jobs or care for infants sleep poorly due to INADEQUATE OPPORTUNITY for sleep; Insomnia Disorder requires difficulty sleeping DESPITE adequate opportunity.\n\nInsomnia vs. Delayed Sleep Phase Disorder: In Delayed Sleep Phase Disorder, the patient cannot sleep at 11 PM but sleeps normally and feels refreshed if allowed to sleep from 3 AM to 11 AM; Insomnia patients sleep poorly regardless of schedule.\n\nInsomnia vs. Normal Age-Related Sleep Changes: Elderly individuals experience physiological reductions in slow-wave sleep and advanced sleep phase; this is not Insomnia Disorder unless accompanied by daytime distress.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 44-year-old manager who reports taking 90 minutes to fall asleep every night and waking at 3:30 AM unable to return to sleep, occurring five nights a week for the past five months.\n\nThe manager has an optimal sleep environment (dark, quiet bedroom, in bed for 8.5 hours) but lies awake feeling anxious about the next day's fatigue. The manager experiences daytime fatigue, irritability, and poor concentration. Meeting criteria for onset and terminal insomnia >=3 nights/week for >3 months despite adequate opportunity, the diagnosis is confirmed as Insomnia Disorder, Persistent. First-line treatment is Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Insomnia Disorder requires sleep dissatisfaction (onset, maintenance, or early morning awakening) occurring at least 3 nights per week for at least 3 months despite ADEQUATE OPPORTUNITY for sleep. DSM-5 eliminated the primary vs. secondary insomnia distinction; insomnia is coded independently even when comorbid with MDD. Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I: sleep restriction, stimulus control) is the gold-standard first-line treatment, superior to long-term sedative-hypnotic medications.",
        'key_concepts' => [
