    3 => [
        'title' => 'Hypersomnolence Disorder & Narcolepsy (Cataplexy & Hypocretin Deficiency)',
        'slug' => 'hypersomnolence-narcolepsy-cataplexy-hypocretin',
        'description' => 'Hypersomnolence criteria (>= 7 hours sleep + daytime sleepiness), Narcolepsy triad (cataplexy, hypocretin deficiency, SOREMPs), and sleep paralysis.',
        'overview' => "Disorders of central hypersomnolence are characterized by excessive daytime sleepiness that is not attributable to inadequate nocturnal sleep duration or disrupted circadian rhythms. The two primary conditions within this category in DSM-5 are Hypersomnolence Disorder and Narcolepsy, which represent radically distinct pathophysiological entities requiring precise clinical differentiation.\n\nHypersomnolence Disorder involves excessive sleepiness despite a main sleep period lasting at least seven hours, accompanied by non-restorative nocturnal sleep, prolonged unrefreshing daytime naps, or sleep inertia ('sleep drunkenness'—prolonged confusion upon awakening). Patients with hypersomnolence sleep long hours (often 10 to 14 hours nightly) yet never feel rested, lacking the abrupt sleep attacks and REM intrusions characteristic of narcolepsy.\n\nIn stark contrast, Narcolepsy is a neurological autoimmune disorder caused by the destruction of approximately 70,000 hypocretin- (orexin-) producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Narcolepsy is characterized by an irrepressible need to sleep, lapses into sleep, or naps occurring within the same day, accompanied by cataplexy (sudden bilateral loss of muscle tone triggered by strong positive emotions such as laughter), hypocretin deficiency in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), or nocturnal polysomnography demonstrating sleep-onset REM periods (SOREMPs).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "For Hypersomnolence Disorder: Criterion A requires self-reported excessive sleepiness (hypersomnolence) despite a main sleep period lasting at least 7 hours, with at least one of the following:\n1. Recurrent periods of sleep or lapses into sleep within the same day.\n2. A prolonged main sleep episode of more than 9 hours per day that is nonrestorative (unrefreshing).\n3. Difficulty being fully awake after abrupt awakening ('sleep inertia').\nCriterion B: Occurs at least 3 times per week for at least 3 months. Criterion C: Causes clinically significant distress or impairment. Criterion D, E, F: Rule out narcolepsy, breathing sleep disorders, substances, and medical conditions.\n\nFor Narcolepsy: Criterion A requires recurrent periods of an irrepressible need to sleep, lapsing into sleep, or napping occurring within the same day, occurring at least 3 times per week over the past 3 months.\nCriterion B mandates the presence of at least ONE of the following three objective markers:\n1. Cataplexy: (a) In longstanding disease, brief (seconds to minutes) episodes of sudden bilateral loss of muscle tone with maintained consciousness that are precipitated by laughter or joking; OR (b) In children/recent onset, spontaneous grimacing or jaw-opening episodes with tongue protrusion or general hypotonia.\n2. Hypocretin Deficiency: CSF hypocretin-1 concentration <= 110 pg/mL (or < 1/3 of normal mean values).\n3. Nocturnal Polysomnography showing REM sleep latency <= 15 minutes, or a daytime MSLT showing mean sleep latency <= 8 minutes and 2 or more sleep-onset REM periods (SOREMPs).",
        'specifiers' => "Narcolepsy Subtypes: (1) Narcolepsy Type 1 (Narcolepsy with cataplexy OR with CSF hypocretin deficiency); (2) Narcolepsy Type 2 (Narcolepsy without cataplexy and without hypocretin deficiency); (3) Narcolepsy secondary to another medical condition (e.g., sarcoidosis, traumatic brain injury).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Narcolepsy vs. Hypersomnolence Disorder: In Narcolepsy, daytime naps are brief (15-30 min), refreshing, and accompanied by cataplexy, sleep paralysis, or hypnagogic hallucinations; in Hypersomnolence Disorder, naps are long (hours), unrefreshing, and cataplexy is ABSENT.\n\nCataplexy vs. Syncope: In cataplexy, consciousness is fully PRESERVED throughout the muscle collapse; in syncope, consciousness is lost.\n\nCataplexy vs. Atonic Seizures: Atonic seizures involve EEG epileptiform discharges without emotional triggers; cataplexy is triggered by laughter or joking.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 19-year-old college student who reports falling asleep uncontrollably during classes for six months. While watching a comedy film with friends, the student experienced sudden bilateral knee buckling and jaw dropping upon laughing, collapsing to the floor while remaining fully awake and aware (cataplexy triggered by laughter).\n\nLumbar puncture reveals CSF hypocretin-1 concentration of 35 pg/mL (profound hypocretin deficiency). Nocturnal PSG followed by MSLT reveals a mean sleep latency of 1.8 minutes with three SOREMPs. The presence of irrepressible sleep attacks with cataplexy, hypocretin deficiency, and SOREMPs confirms Narcolepsy Type 1.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Narcolepsy is caused by autoimmune destruction of hypocretin (orexin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Classic tetrad: (1) Excessive daytime sleepiness, (2) Cataplexy (sudden muscle tone loss triggered by laughter with PRESERVED consciousness), (3) Hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations, (4) Sleep paralysis. Diagnosis requires at least ONE objective marker: cataplexy, CSF hypocretin <= 110 pg/mL, or MSLT with <=8 min latency and 2+ SOREMPs. Sodium oxybate and modafinil are primary pharmacological treatments.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Autoimmune Hypocretin Deficiency', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Narcolepsy Type 1 is caused by selective loss of hypothalamic hypocretin (orexin) neurons regulating REM gating.'],
            ['title' => 'The Cataplexy Laughter Trigger', 'icon' => '😂', 'definition' => 'Bilateral loss of muscle tone precipitated by strong positive emotions (laughter) with completely preserved consciousness.'],
            ['title' => 'Objective Diagnostic Triad', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Requires cataplexy, CSF hypocretin <= 110 pg/mL, OR MSLT sleep latency <= 8 min with 2+ SOREMPs.'],
            ['title' => 'Sleep Inertia in Hypersomnolence', 'icon' => '😴', 'definition' => 'Hypersomnolence Disorder features long, unrefreshing sleep and prolonged morning grogginess (\'sleep drunkenness\').']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Cataplexy', 'definition' => 'Sudden, episodic bilateral loss of skeletal muscle tone provoked by strong positive emotions (laughter, jokes), without loss of consciousness.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypocretin-1 (Orexin-A)', 'definition' => 'A hypothalamic neuropeptide responsible for stabilizing wakefulness; its absence in cerebrospinal fluid confirms Narcolepsy Type 1.'],
            ['term' => 'Sleep Paralysis', 'definition' => 'A temporary inability to move or speak occurring during sleep-wake transitions, resulting from intrusion of REM atonia into wakefulness.'],
            ['term' => 'Sleep Inertia (Sleep Drunkenness)', 'definition' => 'A period of impaired cognitive performance, confusion, and motor ataxia occurring immediately upon awakening from deep sleep.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Parasomnias: Non-REM Sleep Arousal Disorders (Sleepwalking, Sleep Terrors)',
        'slug' => 'parasomnias-nrem-sleep-arousal-disorders',
        'description' => 'NREM arousal disorders (sleepwalking, sleep terrors), Stage N3 delta sleep timing, amnesia for episode, and Nightmare Disorder contrast.',
        'overview' => "Parasomnias are unpleasant or undesirable motor, verbal, or experiential phenomena that occur during entry into sleep, within sleep, or during arousals from sleep. In DSM-5, parasomnias are classified fundamentally by the sleep stage from which they arise: Non-Rapid Eye Movement (NREM) Sleep Arousal Disorders (which emerge from Stage N3 slow-wave sleep) versus REM Sleep Parasomnias (such as Nightmare Disorder and REM Sleep Behavior Disorder).\n\nNon-REM Sleep Arousal Disorders comprise two primary clinical entities: Sleepwalking (Somnambulism) and Sleep Terrors (Pavor Nocturnus). Both conditions arise from an incomplete, dissociated arousal from Stage N3 slow-wave sleep, typically occurring during the first third of the major nocturnal sleep episode when slow-wave delta sleep is concentrated. In this dissociated state, the motor cortex awakens while the cognitive, autobiographical cortex remains trapped in deep delta sleep.\n\nA defining diagnostic hallmark of NREM parasomnias is complete amnesia for the episode and unresponsiveness to comforting during the event. An individual in a sleep terror sits bolt upright with a piercing scream, exhibits intense autonomic arousal (tachycardia, diaphoresis, pupil dilation), and cannot be comforted; in the morning, they have zero recollection of the event or dream imagery.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Criteria for Non-REM Sleep Arousal Disorders:\n\nCriterion A: Recurrent episodes of incomplete awakening from sleep, usually occurring during the first third of the major sleep episode, accompanied by either one of the following:\n1. Sleepwalking: Repeated episodes of rising from bed during sleep and walking about. While sleepwalking, the individual has a blank, staring face; is relatively unresponsive to the efforts of others to communicate with him or her; and can be awakened only with great difficulty.\n2. Sleep Terrors: Recurrent episodes of abrupt terror arousals from sleep, usually beginning with a panicky scream. There is intense fear and signs of autonomic arousal, such as mydriasis, tachycardia, rapid breathing, and sweating, during each episode. There is relative unresponsiveness to efforts of others to comfort the individual during the episodes.\n\nCriterion B: No or little (e.g., only a single visual scene) dream imagery is recalled.\n\nCriterion C: Amnesia for the episodes is present.\n\nCriterion D: The episodes cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion E and F: The disturbance is not attributable to physiological effects of a substance or another medical condition.",
        'specifiers' => "For Sleepwalking, clinicians specify: 'With sleep-related eating' (compulsive nocturnal eating of food or toxic substances without memory) or 'With sleep-related sexual behavior (sexsomnia)'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Sleep Terrors vs. Nightmare Disorder: The classic board examination distinction.\n• Sleep Terrors (NREM): Occur in FIRST THIRD of night (Stage N3 delta); patient is confused/disoriented; COMPLETE AMNESIA; no narrative dream recall; cannot be comforted.\n• Nightmare Disorder (REM): Occur in LAST THIRD of night (REM sleep); patient awakens RAPIDLY ALERT and oriented; VIVID, DETAILED DREAM RECALL; can be comforted.\n\nREM Sleep Behavior Disorder (RBD) vs. Sleepwalking: RBD occurs during REM sleep in the last half of the night; features dream enactment (punching, kicking) due to loss of REM atonia; common precursor to alpha-synuclein neurodegenerative diseases (Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 7-year-old child whose parents report episodes occurring two hours after falling asleep (first third of the night). The child sits up screaming, eyes wide open, sweating profusely with a heart rate of 150 bpm.\n\nWhen parents attempt to hug and console the child, the child pushes them away and screams louder, appearing to look straight through them. After ten minutes, the child lies back down and resumes sleeping. In the morning, the child awakens cheerful with zero memory of the episode or any dreams. Meeting criteria for NREM arousal from Stage N3 with autonomic terror, unresponsiveness to comfort, and morning amnesia, the diagnosis is Non-REM Sleep Arousal Disorder, Sleep terror type.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: NREM Sleep Arousal Disorders (sleepwalking, sleep terrors) occur during Stage N3 slow-wave sleep in the FIRST THIRD of the night, featuring COMPLETE AMNESIA and unresponsiveness to comforting. Nightmare Disorder occurs during REM sleep in the LAST THIRD of the night, featuring RAPID ALERTNESS on awakening and VIVID DREAM RECALL. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder involves loss of REM muscle atonia with dream enactment, strongly predicting future Parkinson's or Lewy body dementia. Reassure parents that pediatric sleep terrors are benign and self-limiting.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Stage N3 First-Third Timing', 'icon' => '🌙', 'definition' => 'NREM arousal disorders emerge from deep slow-wave delta sleep concentrated in the initial third of the night.'],
            ['title' => 'Complete Morning Amnesia', 'icon' => '🕳️', 'definition' => 'Patients have zero recollection of sleep terror episodes or detailed dream imagery upon awakening.'],
            ['title' => 'Sleep Terrors vs. Nightmares Razor', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Terrors = NREM, first third, amnesia, confused. Nightmares = REM, last third, vivid recall, rapidly alert.'],
            ['title' => 'REM Behavior Disorder Synuclein Link', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Enactment of violent dreams due to lost REM atonia strongly predicts Parkinson disease or Lewy body dementia.']
