    5 => [
        'title' => 'The Human Sexual Response Cycle & DSM-5 Diagnostic Framework (6-Month Rule)',
        'slug' => 'human-sexual-response-cycle-dsm5-framework',
        'description' => 'Masters and Johnson / Kaplan 4-phase model, 6-month minimum duration rule, 75%-100% encounter threshold, and non-sexual factor exclusions.',
        'overview' => "Sexual Dysfunctions represent a heterogeneous group of disorders typically characterized by a clinically significant disturbance in an individual's ability to respond sexually or to experience sexual pleasure. In DSM-5, sexual dysfunctions are conceptualized along the physiological continuum of the Human Sexual Response Cycle, historically modeled by William Masters, Virginia Johnson, and Helen Singer Kaplan: (1) Desire (libido), (2) Arousal (excitement/lubrication/erection), (3) Orgasm (ejaculation/climax), and (4) Resolution.\n\nA fundamental structural advancement in DSM-5 was the introduction of strict operational duration and frequency thresholds across all sexual dysfunction diagnoses. In DSM-IV, criteria were vague, relying on subjective clinician judgment of 'persistent or recurrent' deficits, which led to high over-diagnosis of transient sexual mismatches. DSM-5 mandated two universal objective criteria across almost all sexual dysfunctions:\n\n1. The 6-Month Minimum Duration Rule: Symptoms must have persisted for a minimum duration of approximately 6 months.\n2. The 75%-100% Frequency Threshold: The dysfunction must occur on all or almost all (approximately 75%-100%) occasions of sexual activity.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 requires that clinicians systematically rule out non-sexual causes, including severe relationship distress (e.g., intimate partner violence, severe relational hostility) and medical or substance/medication effects (e.g., SSRI-induced sexual dysfunction).",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Universal DSM-5 Sexual Dysfunction Diagnostic Architecture:\n\nCriterion A: Specific dysfunction criteria met across desire, arousal, orgasm, or pelvic pain.\n\nCriterion B: The symptoms have persisted for a minimum duration of approximately 6 months.\n\nCriterion C: The symptoms cause clinically significant distress in the individual.\n\nCriterion D: The sexual dysfunction is not better explained by a non-sexual mental disorder (e.g., major depressive disorder) or as a consequence of severe relationship distress (e.g., partner violence) or other significant stressors, and is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance/medication (e.g., SSRIs, antihypertensives, alcohol) or another medical condition.\n\nUniversal Subtype Coding:\n• Lifelong (the disturbance has been present since the individual became sexually active).\n• Acquired (the disturbance began after a period of relatively normal sexual function).\n• Generalized (not limited to certain types of stimulation, situations, or partners).\n• Situational (only occurs with certain types of stimulation, situations, or partners).",
        'specifiers' => "Every sexual dysfunction is coded with: (1) Lifelong vs. Acquired; (2) Generalized vs. Situational; and (3) Severity grading (Mild, Moderate, Severe based on distress).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Primary Sexual Dysfunction vs. Substance/Medication-Induced Sexual Dysfunction: SSRIs and SNRIs frequently induce delayed ejaculation, anorgasmia, or decreased libido; medication-induced etiology takes precedence if temporal onset coincides with drug initiation.\n\nSexual Dysfunction vs. Relationship Discord: If sexual difficulties occur strictly in the context of intense marital hostility or domestic abuse, a sexual dysfunction is NOT diagnosed.\n\nSexual Dysfunction vs. Major Depressive Disorder: Severe MDD universally blunts sexual desire; if anhedonia and depression are primary, MDD is diagnosed.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 30-year-old man who reports having difficulty maintaining erections. Detailed history reveals that the problem began eight months ago, occurs during 90% of sexual encounters with his partner, causes marked distress, but does not occur during solitary masturbation.\n\nMedical workup confirms normal testosterone and vascular function. There is no major depression or medication use. Meeting criteria for >6 months across >75% of encounters in a partner-specific context, the diagnosis is Erectile Disorder, Acquired, Situational, Moderate Severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: All primary DSM-5 sexual dysfunctions require: (1) minimum duration of approximately 6 months, and (2) occurrence on approximately 75%-100% of sexual encounters. Must rule out relationship violence/hostility, substance/SSRI effects, and medical conditions. Universal subtypes: Lifelong vs. Acquired, and Generalized vs. Situational. Masters & Johnson response cycle: Desire -> Arousal -> Orgasm -> Resolution.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 6-Month Chronicity Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Universal requirement: sexual symptoms must persist for at least 6 months to exclude transient sexual adjustment issues.'],
            ['title' => 'The 75%-100% Frequency Benchmark', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Universal requirement: symptoms must manifest during 75% to 100% of sexual encounters to establish diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Lifelong vs. Acquired Subtypes', 'icon' => '🧬', 'definition' => 'Lifelong indicates presence since initial sexual debut; Acquired indicates onset following normal sexual functioning.'],
            ['title' => 'Generalized vs. Situational Subtypes', 'icon' => '🌍', 'definition' => 'Generalized occurs across all partners and contexts; Situational occurs only with specific partners or stimulation.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Human Sexual Response Cycle', 'definition' => 'The physiological model describing the sequence of physical and emotional changes during sexual stimulation: Desire, Arousal, Orgasm, Resolution.'],
            ['term' => 'SSRI-Induced Sexual Dysfunction', 'definition' => 'An adverse pharmacological effect of serotonergic antidepressants characterized by delayed ejaculation, anorgasmia, or diminished libido.'],
            ['term' => 'Situational Dysfunction', 'definition' => 'A sexual dysfunction that manifests exclusively with specific partners, environments, or types of sexual activity.'],
            ['term' => 'Relational Exclusion', 'definition' => 'The diagnostic mandate that sexual symptoms secondary to severe interpersonal hostility or partner abuse are excluded from psychiatric dysfunction.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Male Sexual Disorders: Delayed Ejaculation & Erectile Disorder',
        'slug' => 'male-sexual-disorders-erectile-ejaculation',
        'description' => 'Delayed Ejaculation, Erectile Disorder, Premature (Early) Ejaculation (the 1-minute criterion), and diagnostic operationalization.',
        'overview' => "Male Sexual Disorders in DSM-5 encompass four discrete diagnostic conditions: Erectile Disorder, Delayed Ejaculation, Premature (Early) Ejaculation, and Hypoactive Sexual Desire Disorder. In DSM-5, these conditions were operationalized with rigorous physiological criteria and temporal benchmarks to distinguish true biological and psychological dysfunctions from occasional variations in sexual performance.\n\nErectile Disorder is characterized by a persistent difficulty in obtaining an erection during sexual activity, maintaining an erection until completion of sexual activity, or a marked decrease in erectile rigidity, occurring on 75%-100% of occasions for at least 6 months. It involves complex interactions between vascular endothelial health, parasympathetic cavernous nerve signaling, and psychological performance anxiety.\n\nA major diagnostic milestone in DSM-5 was the precise quantification of Premature (Early) Ejaculation. In DSM-IV, premature ejaculation was defined vaguely as ejaculation occurring 'sooner than desired'. DSM-5 established an evidence-based objective benchmark: ejaculation that occurs during partnered sexual activity within approximately 1 minute following vaginal penetration and before the individual wishes it, persisting for at least 6 months on 75%-100% of occasions. Delayed Ejaculation represents the opposite phenomenon: marked delay in, or infrequency/absence of, ejaculation despite adequate sexual stimulation.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Criteria for Specific Male Sexual Disorders:\n\n1. Erectile Disorder: On 75%-100% of occasions, experiencing at least one of: (1) Marked difficulty in obtaining an erection during sexual activity; (2) Marked difficulty in maintaining an erection until completion; (3) Marked decrease in erectile rigidity. Duration >= 6 months; marked distress.\n\n2. Premature (Early) Ejaculation: A persistent or recurrent pattern of ejaculation occurring during partnered sexual activity within approximately 1 minute following vaginal penetration and before the individual wishes it. Occurs on 75%-100% of occasions for >= 6 months; causes marked personal distress.\n\n3. Delayed Ejaculation: On 75%-100% of occasions of partnered sexual activity, experiencing either: (1) Marked delay in ejaculation; or (2) Marked infrequency or absence of ejaculation. Duration >= 6 months without the individual desiring delay.\n\n4. Male Hypoactive Sexual Desire Disorder: Persistently or recurrently deficient (or absent) sexual/erotic thoughts or fantasies and desire for sexual activity, taking into account age and sociocultural context, lasting >= 6 months.",
        'specifiers' => "Every male sexual disorder is specified as: Lifelong vs. Acquired; Generalized vs. Situational; and Mild, Moderate, or Severe based on distress level.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Erectile Disorder: Organic (Vascular/Neurogenic) vs. Psychogenic: If nocturnal penile tumescence (morning erections) or solitary masturbatory erections are INTACT, the etiology is predominantly PSYCHOGENIC / SITUATIONAL. If erections are absent under all circumstances including nocturnal sleep, organic vascular or neurological disease is present.\n\nPremature Ejaculation vs. Normal Early Ejaculation: Ejaculation occurring after 3 to 5 minutes of penetration is within normal physiological range, even if the partner desires longer duration; DSM-5 requires <= 1 minute.\n\nDelayed Ejaculation vs. SSRI Adverse Effect: Delayed ejaculation is the most common sexual adverse effect of SSRIs; SSRI-induced etiology takes precedence if drug is present.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 28-year-old married man who reports that during partnered intercourse, he persistently ejaculates within 30 to 45 seconds of vaginal penetration, occurring on every sexual encounter over the past nine months.\n\nThe condition causes severe marital distress and personal embarrassment. There is no medication use, substance use, or organic disease. Meeting the criteria of ejaculation occurring within approximately 1 minute on >75% of occasions for >6 months, the clinical formulation confirms Premature (Early) Ejaculation, Lifelong, Generalized, Severe.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Premature Ejaculation is objectively defined in DSM-5 as ejaculation within approximately 1 minute following penetration on 75%-100% of occasions for at least 6 months. Erectile Disorder: Difficulty obtaining/maintaining rigidity for >= 6 months; intact nocturnal/masturbatory erections prove psychogenic/situational etiology. Delayed Ejaculation requires marked delay or absence of ejaculation; must rule out SSRI side effects. Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors (sildenafil) treat erectile disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 1-Minute Premature Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Objective DSM-5 benchmark: ejaculation occurring within approximately 60 seconds following penetration.'],
            ['title' => 'Nocturnal Tumescence Differentiation', 'icon' => '🌙', 'definition' => 'Presence of normal morning erections definitively separates psychogenic/situational erectile dysfunction from organic vascular failure.'],
            ['title' => 'The 75%-100% Encounter Threshold', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must occur on almost all sexual encounters for at least 6 months to establish formal diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'SSRI Delayed Ejaculation Mimic', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Serotonergic antidepressants delay ejaculation; must rule out medication effects before diagnosing primary Delayed Ejaculation.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Intravaginal Ejaculatory Latency Time (IELT)', 'definition' => 'The objective clinical measurement of time from vaginal intromission to ejaculation, utilized to diagnose Premature Ejaculation (<= 1 minute).'],
            ['term' => 'Nocturnal Penile Tumescence (NPT)', 'definition' => 'Involuntary penile erections occurring during REM sleep, used clinically to differentiate psychogenic from organic erectile dysfunction.'],
            ['term' => 'Phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE-5) Inhibitor', 'definition' => 'A class of medications (sildenafil, tadalafil) that enhance nitric oxide-mediated vasodilation in the corpus cavernosum, treating erectile disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Sensate Focus Therapy', 'definition' => 'A behavioral couples therapy developed by Masters and Johnson involving graduated, non-demanding touch exercises to eliminate sexual performance anxiety.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Female Disorders: Female Orgasmic & Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder',
        'slug' => 'female-sexual-disorders-orgasmic-interest-arousal',
        'description' => 'Female Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder (consolidation of desire and arousal), Female Orgasmic Disorder, and Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder.',
        'overview' => "Female Sexual Disorders in DSM-5 underwent a profound structural consolidation based on modern empirical sexological research. In DSM-IV, female sexual dysfunctions were divided into separate diagnoses for Hypoactive Sexual Desire Disorder and Female Sexual Arousal Disorder, mirroring the male sexual response cycle. However, extensive clinical research led by Rosemary Basson demonstrated that female sexual response does not follow a linear progression from desire to arousal; in women, sexual desire and subjective/physiological arousal are profoundly overlapping and mutually reinforcing.\n\nConsequently, DSM-5 merged these historical categories into a single unified diagnosis: Female Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder (FSIAD). FSIAD captures absent or significantly reduced sexual interest, erotic thoughts, responsive desire, and genital or non-genital sensations on 75%-100% of occasions for at least 6 months.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 created Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder (GPPPD), which consolidated DSM-IV Vaginismus (involuntary pelvic muscle spasm) and Dyspareunia (painful intercourse) into a single condition, recognizing that pelvic muscle tension and pain cannot be reliably separated in clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Criteria for Specific Female Sexual Disorders:\n\n1. Female Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder (FSIAD): Lack of, or significantly reduced, sexual interest/arousal, manifested by at least THREE of the following six criteria on 75%-100% of occasions for >= 6 months:\n• Absent/reduced interest in sexual activity.\n• Absent/reduced sexual/erotic thoughts or fantasies.\n• No/reduced initiation of sexual activity, and typically unreceptive to a partner's attempts to initiate.\n• Absent/reduced sexual excitement/pleasure during sexual activity on almost all encounters.\n• Absent/reduced sexual interest/arousal in response to any internal or external sexual/erotic cues.\n• Absent/reduced genital or nongenital sensations during sexual activity on almost all encounters.\n\n2. Female Orgasmic Disorder: Marked delay in, marked infrequency of, or absence of orgasm, OR markedly reduced intensity of orgasmic sensations, occurring on 75%-100% of occasions for >= 6 months, causing marked distress. (Note: A woman who can achieve orgasm with clitoral stimulation but not through coitus alone is within normal physiological limits and is NOT diagnosed with Female Orgasmic Disorder).\n\n3. Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder (GPPPD): Persistent or recurrent difficulties for >= 6 months with at least one of: (1) Marked vulvovaginal or pelvic pain during penetration; (2) Marked fear/anxiety about pain in anticipation of penetration; (3) Marked tensing/tightening of pelvic floor muscles during attempted penetration; (4) Vaginal penetration difficulty.",
        'specifiers' => "Every disorder is specified: Lifelong vs. Acquired; Generalized vs. Situational; and Mild, Moderate, or Severe based on distress level.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Normal Clitoral Orgasmic Variation vs. Female Orgasmic Disorder: Over 70% of women require clitoral stimulation to achieve orgasm; lack of coital orgasm in the presence of normal clitoral orgasm is physiologically normal and must NOT be pathologized.\n\nGPPPD vs. Vulvovaginal Medical Conditions: Lichen sclerosus, vulvar vestibulitis, endometriosis, and estrogen deficiency (atrophic vaginitis) must be ruled out through gynecological examination.\n\nFSIAD vs. Marital Discord: Decreased sexual interest secondary to partner conflict is excluded.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 26-year-old woman who presents with severe distress regarding sexual intercourse. For the past year, whenever vaginal penetration is attempted by her partner, she experiences intense fear of pain and involuntary, severe spastic tightening of her pelvic floor muscles, completely preventing penetration.\n\nGynecological examination reveals no infection, no endometriosis, and normal anatomical structures. The presence of marked pelvic muscle tensing, fear of pain, and inability to achieve vaginal penetration for >6 months confirms Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder (GPPPD). Treatment incorporates pelvic floor physical therapy and graduated vaginal dilator therapy.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: DSM-5 merged female desire and arousal into Female Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder (requires 3 of 6 criteria for >= 6 months). DSM-5 merged Vaginismus and Dyspareunia into Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder (GPPPD). Female Orgasmic Disorder requires marked delay/absence of orgasm on 75%-100% of occasions; lack of orgasm from coitus alone in the presence of clitoral orgasm is NORMAL and not a disorder. Treatment for GPPPD: pelvic physical therapy and graduated dilators.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Desire & Arousal Consolidation', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Merged Hypoactive Desire and Arousal disorders into FSIAD, reflecting non-linear female sexual neurobiology.'],
            ['title' => 'The GPPPD Consolidation', 'icon' => '🩺', 'definition' => 'Consolidated Vaginismus (spasm) and Dyspareunia (pain) into Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder.'],
            ['title' => 'Normal Clitoral Orgasm Principle', 'icon' => '🌸', 'definition' => 'Requiring clitoral stimulation for orgasm is normal physiological variation and does not warrant diagnosis.'],
            ['title' => 'Graduated Dilator Protocol', 'icon' => '🛠️', 'definition' => 'Behavioral desensitization using progressive vaginal dilators alongside pelvic physical therapy for GPPPD.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'FSIAD', 'definition' => 'Female Sexual Interest/Arousal Disorder; a DSM-5 diagnosis combining deficiencies in female sexual desire, subjective excitement, and physiological lubrication.'],
            ['term' => 'GPPPD', 'definition' => 'Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder; a condition characterized by vulvovaginal pain, pelvic muscle tightening, and fear during attempted penetration.'],
            ['term' => 'Basson\'s Non-Linear Model', 'definition' => 'A circular model of female sexual response proposing that emotional intimacy and receptive desire precede arousal and spontaneous hunger.'],
            ['term' => 'Vaginal Dilator Therapy', 'definition' => 'A graduated physical and behavioral desensitization treatment utilizing progressive smooth cylinders to relax pelvic floor hypertonicity.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Gender Dysphoria in Children (Criterion A: 6 of 8 Indicators, 6-Month Duration)',
        'slug' => 'gender-dysphoria-children-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 6 of 8 indicator threshold, mandatory cross-gender desire, 6-month duration, and distinction from gender nonconformity.',
        'overview' => "Gender Dysphoria represents a distinct diagnostic category in DSM-5, replacing the historical DSM-IV diagnosis of 'Gender Identity Disorder'. This nomenclature and conceptual shift reflects a fundamental paradigm evolution in modern psychiatry: gender nonconformity or having a gender identity that differs from assigned sex at birth is not a mental disorder in itself. The psychiatric diagnosis requires the presence of clinically significant distress or functional impairment resulting from the marked incongruence between one's experienced/expressed gender and one's assigned gender at birth.\n\nIn children, diagnostic criteria were made substantially more rigorous in DSM-5 to prevent pathologizing normal developmental gender-nonconforming play or transient tomboyish/sissy behavior. While DSM-IV allowed diagnosis based primarily on preferences for toys and playmates, DSM-5 requires a persistent, pervasive constellation of behaviors meeting at least six of eight specific criteria lasting for at least six months.\n\nCrucially, under Criterion A1, at least one of the six qualifying criteria must be a strong desire to be of the other gender or an insistence that one is the other gender. A child who merely plays with opposite-gender toys or prefers opposite-gender playmates without insisting on being the other gender does NOT meet criteria for Gender Dysphoria.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A marked incongruence between one's experienced/expressed gender and assigned gender, of at least 6 months' duration, as manifested by at least six of the following (one of which MUST be Criterion A1):\n1. A strong desire to be of the other gender or an insistence that one is the other gender (or some alternative gender different from assigned gender) - MANDATORY CORE CRITERION.\n2. In boys (assigned sex), a strong preference for cross-dressing or simulating female attire; or in girls (assigned sex), a strong preference for wearing only typical masculine clothing and a strong resistance to wearing typical feminine clothing.\n3. A strong preference for cross-gender roles in make-believe play or fantasy play.\n4. A strong preference for the toys, games, or activities stereotypically used or engaged in by the other gender.\n5. A strong preference for playmates of the other gender.\n6. In boys (assigned sex), a strong rejection of typically masculine toys, games, and activities and a marked avoidance of rough-and-tumble play; or in girls (assigned sex), a strong rejection of typically feminine toys, games, and activities.\n7. A strong dislike of one's sexual anatomy.\n8. A strong desire for the primary and/or secondary sex characteristics that match one's experienced gender.\n\nCriterion B: The condition is associated with clinically significant distress or impairment in social, school, or other important areas of functioning.",
        'specifiers' => "Specifier: 'With a disorder of sex development' (coded if the child has a concurrent congenital disorder of sex development, such as congenital adrenal hyperplasia or androgen insensitivity syndrome).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Gender Dysphoria in Children vs. Gender Nonconformity: Normal gender-nonconforming play (e.g., a girl playing with trucks, or a boy dressing in princess costumes) lacks the mandatory Criterion A1 insistence that they ARE the other gender and does not cause distress.\n\nGender Dysphoria vs. Autism Spectrum Disorder: ASD children frequently exhibit gender-nonconforming interests; careful developmental history is required to determine whether gender incongruence is persistent.\n\nGender Dysphoria vs. Body Dysmorphic Disorder: In BDD, the distress targets perceived ugliness or flaws of specific body parts (nose, skin); in Gender Dysphoria, distress targets sex characteristics that do not match gender identity.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 6-year-old child assigned male at birth who, since age 3, consistently insists: 'I am a girl, not a boy' (Criterion A1 satisfied). The child wears only dresses, adopts mother/princess roles in fantasy play, plays exclusively with female peers, refuses masculine toys, and cries during bath time, expressing intense disgust with having a penis (Criteria A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, A7 satisfied).\n\nThe child experiences intense school distress and isolation. Meeting 7 of 8 criteria for over three years (well exceeding the 6-month threshold) with the mandatory Criterion A1, the clinical formulation confirms Gender Dysphoria in Children.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Gender Dysphoria requires marked incongruence between experienced gender and assigned sex lasting >= 6 months, accompanied by clinically significant distress. In children, requires at least 6 of 8 indicators, and Criterion A1 (strong desire to be the other gender or insistence that one IS the other gender) is MANDATORY. Gender nonconformity without desire to be the other gender is NOT a disorder. Replaced DSM-IV Gender Identity Disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Mandatory Criterion A1 Anchor', 'icon' => '⚓', 'definition' => 'Requires a strong desire to be of the other gender or insistence that one IS the other gender; toys alone do not qualify.'],
            ['title' => 'The 6 of 8 Indicator Threshold', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Pediatric diagnosis requires at least 6 distinct behavioral markers persisting continuously for at least 6 months.'],
            ['title' => 'De-Pathologization of Nonconformity', 'icon' => '🌈', 'definition' => 'Gender nonconformity and cross-gender expression are not disorders; diagnosis requires clinically significant distress.'],
            ['title' => 'Disorder of Sex Development Specifier', 'icon' => '🧬', 'definition' => 'Applied when gender dysphoria coexists with documented genetic/endocrine intersex conditions.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Experienced/Expressed Gender', 'definition' => 'An individual\'s deeply felt, internal psychological sense of their own gender identity, which may align or differ from assigned sex.'],
            ['term' => 'Assigned Gender at Birth', 'definition' => 'The initial administrative classification of an infant as male or female based on physical external genital anatomy at birth.'],
            ['term' => 'Gender Nonconformity', 'definition' => 'The extent to which a person\'s gender identity, role, or expression differs from cultural norms prescribed for people of a particular sex.'],
            ['term' => 'Disorder of Sex Development (DSD)', 'definition' => 'Congenital conditions in which development of chromosomal, gonadal, or anatomical sex is atypical (intersex conditions).']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Gender Dysphoria in Adolescents and Adults (Criterion A: 2 of 6 Indicators)',
        'slug' => 'gender-dysphoria-adolescents-adults-criteria',
        'description' => 'The 2 of 6 indicator threshold, pubertal secondary sex characteristics distress, post-transition specifier, and medical affirmation.',
        'overview' => "Gender Dysphoria in Adolescents and Adults represents the mature developmental manifestation of gender incongruence. In adolescents and adults, the onset or acute exacerbation of gender dysphoria is frequently precipitated by the physical emergence of secondary sexual characteristics during puberty (e.g., breast development, menstruation, voice deepening, facial hair growth, penile enlargement), which confronts the individual with undeniable anatomical divergence from their experienced gender.\n\nReflecting the greater cognitive and communicative maturity of adolescents and adults, the diagnostic symptom threshold is significantly different from the pediatric criteria: while children require six of eight criteria, adolescents and adults require only two of six indicators, persisting for at least six months.\n\nThese criteria focus on: (1) marked incongruence between experienced gender and primary/secondary sex characteristics; (2) strong desire to be rid of one's sex characteristics; (3) strong desire for the sex characteristics of the other gender; (4) strong desire to be of the other gender; (5) strong desire to be treated as the other gender; and (6) strong conviction of having the typical feelings and reactions of the other gender. Furthermore, DSM-5 includes a specific 'post-transition' specifier for individuals who have completed social and medical transition.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: A marked incongruence between one's experienced/expressed gender and assigned gender, of at least 6 months' duration, as manifested by at least two of the following:\n1. A marked incongruence between one's experienced/expressed gender and primary and/or secondary sex characteristics (or in young adolescents, the anticipated secondary sex characteristics).\n2. A strong desire to be rid of one's primary and/or secondary sex characteristics because of a marked incongruence with experienced/expressed gender (or in young adolescents, a desire to prevent anticipated characteristics).\n3. A strong desire for the primary and/or secondary sex characteristics of the other gender.\n4. A strong desire to be of the other gender (or some alternative gender different from assigned gender).\n5. A strong desire to be treated as the other gender (or some alternative gender different from assigned gender).\n6. A strong conviction that one has the typical feelings and reactions of the other gender (or some alternative gender different from assigned gender).\n\nCriterion B: The condition is associated with clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic Specifiers:\n• 'With a disorder of sex development' (if a congenital DSD/intersex condition is present).\n• 'Post-transition': Applied if the individual has transitioned to full-time living in the desired gender (with or without legal sex designation change) and has undergone (or is preparing to undergo) at least one cross-sex medical procedure or treatment (hormone therapy or surgery).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Gender Dysphoria vs. Transvestic Disorder: In Transvestic Disorder, cross-dressing generates intense sexual arousal (paraphilic autogynephilia) without a desire to be of the other gender in daily life; in Gender Dysphoria, cross-dressing is for gender identity affirmation and is NOT sexually arousing.\n\nGender Dysphoria vs. Body Dysmorphic Disorder: In BDD, the defect is perceived as ugly/deformed; in Gender Dysphoria, the body parts are recognized as normal male/female anatomy that does not match experienced gender.\n\nGender Dysphoria vs. Schizophrenia: Rarely, a psychotic delusion involves somatic delusions of changing sex; in schizophrenia, the belief is bizarre and accompanied by hallucinations and thought disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 18-year-old assigned female at birth who presents to an adolescent health clinic reporting severe dysphoria since early puberty. The individual binds their chest to flatten breast tissue, experiences distress over menstruation, and desires testosterone therapy and top surgery to achieve a masculine physique (Criteria A1, A2, A3 satisfied).\n\nThe individual has lived socially as a male for two years, uses he/him pronouns, and feels profound relief when addressed as a man (Criteria A4, A5 satisfied). The condition causes severe depression and anxiety. Meeting 5 of the 6 criteria for >6 months with marked distress, the clinical formulation confirms Gender Dysphoria in Adolescents and Adults.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Gender Dysphoria in Adolescents and Adults requires at least 2 of 6 criteria (compared to 6 of 8 in children) persisting for at least 6 months. Focus is on incongruence with primary/secondary sex characteristics, desire to be rid of them, and desire for opposite sex characteristics. Differentiate from Transvestic Disorder (where cross-dressing is for sexual arousal). The 'Post-transition' specifier applies to individuals who have transitioned full-time and undergone hormone or surgical affirmation.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 2 of 6 Adult Threshold', 'icon' => '✌️', 'definition' => 'Adolescent and adult diagnosis requires only 2 of 6 indicators (unlike children who require 6 of 8).'],
            ['title' => 'Secondary Sex Characteristic Focus', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Dysphoria is heavily focused on pubertal physical anatomy: breasts, menses, facial hair, voice, and genitalia.'],
            ['title' => 'The Post-Transition Specifier', 'icon' => '🏷️', 'definition' => 'Assigned when an individual has completed social transition and undergone gender-affirming medical treatments.'],
            ['title' => 'Transvestic Paraphilia Boundary', 'icon' => '👗', 'definition' => 'Transvestic disorder involves paraphilic sexual arousal from cross-dressing, which is absent in gender dysphoria.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Gender Incongruence', 'definition' => 'The objective divergence between an individual\'s experienced gender identity and their assigned biological sex at birth.'],
            ['term' => 'Chest Binding', 'definition' => 'The practice of flattening breast tissue using constrictive garments to achieve a more masculine chest contour.'],
            ['term' => 'Gender-Affirming Hormone Therapy (GAHT)', 'definition' => 'The medical administration of testosterone or estrogen/anti-androgens to align secondary sex characteristics with experienced gender.'],
            ['term' => 'Social Transition', 'definition' => 'The interpersonal process of adopting one\'s experienced gender role, including name, pronouns, attire, and hair styling in public life.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Board-Exam Synthesis',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-exam-synthesis-sleep-sexual-gender',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Sleep-Wake, Sexual Dysfunctions, and Gender Dysphoria under DSM-5 criteria, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Sleep-Wake Disorders, Sexual Dysfunctions, and Gender Dysphoria under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. These three specialized chapters represent heavily tested content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over physiological parameters, diagnostic criteria, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Insomnia Disorder, Narcolepsy, NREM Parasomnias vs. Nightmare Disorder, Erectile Disorder, Premature Ejaculation, FSIAD, GPPPD, and Gender Dysphoria across pediatric and adult cohorts. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the uncoupling of insomnia from secondary status, the 6-month and 75%-100% universal sexual dysfunction rules, the objective 1-minute premature ejaculation criterion, and the de-pathologization of gender nonconformity.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and evidence-based neurobiological mechanisms, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Diagnostic Rules:\n\n1. Insomnia Disorder: Sleep dissatisfaction (onset, maintenance, early awakening) >= 3 nights/week for >= 3 months despite ADEQUATE OPPORTUNITY for sleep. Standalone diagnosis.\n\n2. Narcolepsy: Irrepressible need to sleep + >= 1 objective marker: Cataplexy (sudden muscle tone loss triggered by laughter with PRESERVED consciousness), CSF hypocretin <= 110 pg/mL, or MSLT with <=8 min latency and 2+ SOREMPs.\n\n3. Sleep Terrors vs. Nightmares: Terrors = Stage N3 NREM, first third of night, complete amnesia, confused. Nightmares = REM sleep, last third of night, vivid recall, rapidly alert.\n\n4. Universal Sexual Dysfunction Rules: Symptoms must persist for >= 6 months on approximately 75%-100% of occasions; rule out relational violence, SSRIs, and medical disease.\n\n5. Premature Ejaculation: Ejaculation within approximately 1 minute following vaginal penetration on 75%-100% of occasions for >= 6 months.\n\n6. Female Consolidations: FSIAD merges desire and arousal (3 of 6 criteria). GPPPD merges vaginismus and dyspareunia.\n\n7. Gender Dysphoria: In children, requires >= 6 of 8 indicators with Criterion A1 MANDATORY (desire to be other gender). In adolescents/adults, requires >= 2 of 6 indicators.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Subtypes in Narcolepsy (Type 1 with cataplexy/hypocretin vs. Type 2); Universal sexual subtypes (Lifelong vs. Acquired; Generalized vs. Situational); Post-transition specifier in Gender Dysphoria.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Patient takes 90 min to sleep 4 nights/wk for 4 months with adequate opportunity -> Insomnia Disorder (CBT-I first-line).\n• Knees buckle upon laughing with full consciousness -> Cataplexy -> Narcolepsy Type 1 (hypocretin deficiency).\n• Screams 2 hours after sleeping, thrashing, cannot be comforted, no memory in morning -> Sleep Terror (NREM Stage N3).\n• Wakes up rapidly alert from detailed terrifying dream in early morning -> Nightmare Disorder (REM sleep).\n• Ejaculates in 40 seconds on every encounter for 8 months -> Premature Ejaculation (1-minute rule).\n• Pain and involuntary pelvic muscle tightening preventing penetration -> Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder.\n• Child insists she is a girl, plays with dolls, cries over having a penis -> Gender Dysphoria in Children (A1 mandatory).",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 22-year-old college student who collapses to the floor whenever she hears a hilarious joke, remaining fully awake while her arms and legs become limp for 60 seconds. Overnight polysomnography followed by daytime MSLT reveals a mean sleep latency of 3 minutes and three sleep-onset REM periods (SOREMPs).\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the underlying pathophysiology? The candidate recognizes that the symptom is cataplexy and the PSG findings confirm Narcolepsy Type 1, which is caused by the autoimmune destruction of hypocretin (orexin) producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: Insomnia = 3 nights/wk for 3 mos despite adequate opportunity. Narcolepsy = hypocretin deficiency, cataplexy (laughter trigger, conscious), SOREMPs. Sleep terrors = NREM Stage N3, first third, amnesia. Nightmares = REM, last third, vivid recall. Sexual dysfunctions = 6 months, 75%-100% encounters. Premature ejaculation = 1 minute. FSIAD = 3 of 6 criteria. GPPPD = pain/muscle spasm. Gender Dysphoria = children (6 of 8, A1 mandatory); adults (2 of 6).",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Sleep, Sex & Gender Master Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of physiological parameters, duration thresholds, and operational definitions across three clinical domains.'],
            ['title' => 'The Hypocretin Narcolepsy Mechanism', 'icon' => '🔬', 'definition' => 'Understanding hypothalamic hypocretin loss and its manifestation as cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and REM intrusion.'],
            ['title' => 'Universal Sexual Dysfunction Rules', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'The 6-month duration floor and 75%-100% frequency threshold applied across all primary sexual disorders.'],
